; Venus — the names-are-0-many theorem without a cross-language step.
; German holds two everyday names for the one body at the same time: `die Venus` and
; `der Morgenstern` / `der Abendstern`. Feminine and masculine, one language, one
; speaker, one evening. The compound takes the gender of its head noun `Stern`, so the
; gender is computed from the composition's head and the referent is never consulted.
; Five digits, low to high: Venus acks feminine; Morgenstern acks masculine; two names
; of one referent disagree inside German; the two Stern compounds share a head so they
; share a gender; and they are nonetheless two different cells — agreeing on gender
; only through the head they share, never through the body they both point at.
